@@ PE1 | Agile
Which technique would help the team and stakeholders see a fan's experience of buying match tickets from start to finish?
A) A customer journey map
B) A work breakdown structure
C) A resource histogram
D) A risk register
= A
? A customer journey map shows each step of the customer's experience, including pain points, which gives everyone the same picture of what needs to improve.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
At the kickoff of an offshore wind project, which message best sets a shared foundation for the work?
A) Safety comes first, and everyone has the authority to stop unsafe work
B) The schedule comes first, since the installation vessels are very expensive
C) The budget comes first, since the investors expect a high financial return
D) Each contractor should focus only on its own work package and its own goals
= A
? In high-risk work, a shared commitment to safety, including everyone's authority to stop unsafe work, is the foundation for all other goals.

@@ PE1 | Agile
Lawyers and developers use the same terms with different meanings, which causes rework. What should the team do?
A) Build a shared glossary of legal terms and use it in stories and conversations
B) Ask the lawyers to avoid legal terms completely when they talk to the developers
C) Let the developers interpret the terms as they think best and correct them later
D) Ask the product owner to translate every conversation between the two groups
= A
? A shared vocabulary, used consistently in conversations, stories and code, creates common understanding between domain experts and developers.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
The food plant's project aims to reduce waste. How can the team make this goal meaningful for everyone?
A) Turn it into a measurable target, such as halving waste in a year, and show progress
B) Ask each shift to reduce waste as much as it can, without setting a specific target for the plant
C) Leave the goal general, so that each department can interpret it in the way that suits it best
D) Discuss the goal only with the plant's managers, since they are responsible for the results
= A
? A specific, measurable and visible goal gives people a shared target and lets them see their contribution.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
Which part of the project charter explains why the project is being undertaken?
A) The project purpose
B) The milestone schedule
C) The summary budget
D) The list of stakeholders
= A
? The charter's purpose or justification explains why the project exists, often by referring to the business case.

@@ PE1 | Agile
Which tool summarises a product's target group, their needs, the product and the business goals on a single page?
A) A product vision board
B) A sprint burndown chart
C) A RACI chart
D) A network diagram
= A
? A product vision board captures the vision, target group, needs, product and business goals, which helps align the team and stakeholders.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid | multi
Which two practices help new team members quickly understand the project's purpose? (Choose two.)
A) An onboarding session covering the vision and goals
B) Access to the charter and roadmap, with a buddy to discuss them
C) Reading all of the project's emails from the past year
D) Waiting for them to pick up the purpose on the job
E) Studying the detailed budget spreadsheet in their first week
= A, B
? Structured onboarding and access to the key documents, with someone to discuss them, quickly connect newcomers to the project's purpose.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
Local fishermen oppose the offshore wind farm's cable route, which crosses their fishing grounds. What should the project manager do?
A) Meet them to understand their interests and explore options, such as compensation
B) Proceed with the approved route, since the project has all of the permits that it needs
C) Ask the authorities to keep the fishermen away from the cable route during construction
D) Change the route immediately to avoid any further conflict with the fishermen
= A
? Understanding interests rather than positions opens up options that can meet the needs of both sides.

@@ PE2 | Agile
A developer gets upset whenever teammates change code that he wrote. What should the scrum master do?
A) Discuss his concerns and reinforce the team's collective code ownership
B) Ask the other developers not to change his code without his written permission
C) Assign all changes to his code to him, so that nobody else needs to touch it
D) Ignore the reaction, since he will get used to others changing his code eventually
= A
? Collective ownership reduces bottlenecks and knowledge silos. Discussing the concern helps the developer accept it, for example through reviews or pairing.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
The production manager wants to ship a batch with borderline test results to meet an order, but the quality manager refuses. What should the project manager do?
A) Support the food safety procedures and escalate the commercial impact if needed
B) Ship the batch, since the test results are only borderline and the customer is waiting for it
C) Ask the two managers to compromise by shipping half of the batch to the customer
D) Leave the decision to the production manager, since production is accountable for orders
= A
? Safety and compliance procedures are not negotiable. The project manager supports them and escalates the business consequences to the right level.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
Two sponsors of a sports event each claim exclusive rights to branding at the main entrance. What should the project manager do?
A) Review the two contracts and negotiate a solution based on what each one actually grants
B) Give the entrance to the sponsor that paid more for its sponsorship package
C) Divide the entrance equally between the two sponsors to keep both of them satisfied
D) Remove all branding from the entrance so that neither of the sponsors has any reason to complain
= A
? Contractual disputes are resolved by referring to what the contracts say and negotiating from there.

@@ PE2 | Agile
A disagreement between two developers is escalating through a long chain of chat messages. What should the scrum master suggest?
A) A direct conversation, by call or in person
B) More detailed chat messages with evidence
C) A vote by the whole team on who is right
D) A formal complaint to their line managers
= A
? Text-based arguments easily escalate. A direct conversation allows tone, clarification and quicker resolution.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
To save money, the client asks the project to remove offshore survival training for technicians. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain that the training is a legal and safety requirement, and look for savings elsewhere
B) Remove the training, since the client is paying for the project and makes the final decisions
C) Shorten the training to one hour, so that the client saves some money and the requirement is partly met
D) Ask the technicians to pay for the survival training themselves if they want to take it
= A
? Safety and legal requirements are not traded away. The project manager explains the constraint and helps the client find other savings.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
Team members from different cultures view deadlines differently: some see them as firm commitments, others as flexible targets. Friction is growing. What should the project manager do?
A) Discuss the different expectations openly and agree explicit team norms for deadlines
B) Assume that everyone sees deadlines the way the project manager sees them
C) Ask the team members who see deadlines as flexible to change their attitude
D) Avoid discussing the topic, since cultural differences are a sensitive matter for many people
= A
? Making cultural assumptions explicit and agreeing shared norms prevents recurring conflict in diverse teams.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid | multi
Which two techniques help to de-escalate a heated conflict? (Choose two.)
A) Listening actively to each side
B) Focusing on shared interests
C) Assigning blame quickly
D) Raising your voice to regain control
E) Postponing the discussion indefinitely
= A, B
? Active listening reduces emotional intensity, and a focus on shared interests moves the discussion towards solutions.

@@ PE3 | Agile
Developers find their bug-fixing work dull, and morale is dropping. What should the scrum master do?
A) Rotate the work, share its impact on users and let the team fix the causes of bugs
B) Offer a bonus for every bug fixed, so that the developers are motivated to fix as many bugs as possible
C) Assign all of the bug-fixing work to the newest team members, who have less experience
D) Tell the developers that bug-fixing is part of the job and that their morale is their own responsibility
= A
? Variety, purpose and the autonomy to fix root causes increase motivation more than rewards for output.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
Offshore technicians work 12-hour shifts for 14 days in a row. What should the project manager ensure?
A) Rotation and rest rules that manage fatigue, in line with regulations
B) Additional overtime whenever the weather is good, to make the most of each weather window
C) Shorter breaks, so that more work can be done during each shift
D) A bonus for technicians who work extra days without a rest period
= A
? Fatigue is a serious safety risk. Leaders plan work patterns that comply with regulations and protect people's health.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
Senior lawyers question the authority of a project manager who is not a lawyer. What should the project manager do?
A) Build credibility through competence, listening and keeping commitments, and clarify roles
B) Insist on being obeyed, since the project manager has been formally appointed to lead the project
C) Let the senior lawyers make all of the project's decisions, since they are the experts in their field
D) Avoid the senior lawyers and work only with junior staff, who are more willing to cooperate
= A
? Influence in expert organisations comes from credibility and relationships, supported by clearly agreed roles.

@@ PE3 | Predictive | match
Match each type of power with its source.
1) Legitimate
2) Expert
3) Referent
4) Coercive
A) A formal position in the organisation
B) Knowledge and skills
C) Others' respect for, and identification with, the person
D) The ability to impose penalties
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? Project managers often rely more on expert and referent power than on formal or coercive power, which tends to produce compliance rather than commitment.

@@ PE3 | Agile
The team always asks the scrum master to talk to stakeholders on its behalf. What should the scrum master do?
A) Encourage and coach team members to engage stakeholders directly
B) Continue to act as the go-between, since this protects the team
C) Ask the product owner to handle every contact with stakeholders
D) Stop all contact between the team and stakeholders for now
= A
? Direct collaboration between developers and stakeholders improves understanding. The scrum master builds the team's confidence to do it.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
After automation, several line workers are promoted to shift supervisors, but they have no leadership experience. What should the project do?
A) Provide leadership training and mentoring for the new supervisors
B) Let them learn on the job, since they know the line very well
C) Ask the plant manager to supervise all of the shifts personally
D) Replace them with external supervisors who have experience
= A
? People moving into new roles need development support. Training and mentoring help them succeed and sustain the change.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
At the end of the project, the team is being disbanded, and members feel uncertain about what comes next. Which stage of Tuckman's model is this?
A) Adjourning
B) Norming
C) Storming
D) Performing
= A
? Adjourning is the final stage, when the team completes its work and members move on. Recognition and support for next steps help at this stage.

@@ PE3 | Agile | multi
Which two practices build psychological safety in a team? (Choose two.)
A) Leaders admitting their own mistakes
B) Responding to bad news with curiosity, not blame
C) Punishing errors in front of the team
D) Letting only senior people speak in meetings
E) Hiding problems from senior management
= A, B
? Psychological safety grows when leaders model openness and react to problems by learning rather than blaming.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
As a new team becomes more capable and committed, how should the leader's style change?
A) From more directive towards more delegating
B) From delegating towards more directive
C) It should stay the same throughout the project
D) From supportive towards more controlling
= A
? Situational leadership adapts to the team's development: as competence and commitment grow, the leader gives less direction and more autonomy.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
The wind farm needs a connection to the electricity grid. Which stakeholder's timeline is critical?
A) The grid operator's, since its connection works must align with commissioning
B) The local tourist office's, since visitors may want to see the turbines
C) The turbine supplier's marketing department's, since it plans press releases about the project
D) The project team's social committee's, for the completion celebration
= A
? A grid connection is an external dependency. The grid operator is a key stakeholder whose plans must align with the project's.

@@ PE4 | Agile
The law firm's clients will use a new portal to track their cases. How should the clients be engaged during development?
A) Involve a few representative clients in early testing and feedback sessions
B) Ask the partners to describe what the clients will need from the portal
C) Launch the portal to all clients at once and collect complaints afterwards
D) Engage the clients only after the portal has been fully completed
= A
? Involving representative end users early gives direct feedback and builds support, while managing confidentiality.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
Major supermarket customers audit the food plant's suppliers. How should the project treat these customers?
A) As key stakeholders whose requirements and audit schedules are built into the plan
B) As outside parties, since they are not involved in the plant's internal projects
C) As stakeholders who should be informed only after the project has finished and been audited
D) As competitors, since they might use the information against the plant later
= A
? Customers whose requirements affect the project are key stakeholders. Their standards and audits shape the scope and schedule.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
A football club plans to replace standing areas in its stadium with seats. How should fan groups be engaged?
A) Consult them early through structured forums, explaining the options and constraints
B) Inform them once the decision has been made and the construction work has started
C) Avoid engaging them, since fan groups always oppose any changes to the club's stadium
D) Let the club's marketing team handle all of the questions on social media
= A
? Early, structured consultation lets affected groups influence decisions and understand the constraints, which builds acceptance.

@@ PE4 | Agile
How often should an agile team revisit its stakeholder map?
A) Regularly, for example each quarter or when the context changes
B) Once, at the start of the project, since stakeholders do not change
C) Only when a stakeholder complains about the way the project is run
D) Never, since agile teams do not use stakeholder maps at all
= A
? Stakeholders and their interests change. Revisiting the map regularly keeps engagement relevant.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
The project manager wants to classify stakeholders by their power, their interest and their attitude (support or resistance). Which model fits best?
A) A stakeholder cube
B) A Pareto chart
C) A Kano model
D) A control chart
= A
? A stakeholder cube combines several dimensions, such as power, interest and attitude, to refine the analysis beyond a two-dimensional grid.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
Offshore lifting operations need approval from a marine warranty surveyor appointed by the insurers. What should the project manager do?
A) Engage the surveyor early and plan the approvals for each critical operation
B) Contact the surveyor only when a lifting operation is about to begin
C) Carry out the lifts without approval if the weather window is short, and inform the surveyor later
D) Ask the vessel's captain to approve the lifts instead of the surveyor
= A
? Third parties with approval authority are key stakeholders. Their involvement and approvals are planned to avoid delays and uninsured operations.

@@ PE4 | Agile | multi
Which two signs indicate that stakeholders are becoming disengaged? (Choose two.)
A) Falling attendance at sprint reviews
B) Little or no feedback on increments
C) Many questions during reviews
D) Active participation in refinement
E) Frequent requests for demos
= A, B
? Declining attendance and feedback are early warning signs of disengagement that should prompt action.

@@ PE5 | Agile
Partners expect the new AI drafting tool to remove the need for lawyers to review documents. What should the product owner do?
A) Explain the tool's limits and agree that lawyers must still review its output
B) Confirm the expectation, since the AI tool has been trained on thousands of legal documents
C) Avoid the topic until the partners have used the tool and seen its limits for themselves
D) Promise that the next version of the tool will remove the need for any review at all
= A
? AI tools can make mistakes. Expectations are aligned around human accountability and review of AI output.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
Investors expect "first power" on a specific date, but the installation depends on weather windows. What should the project manager do?
A) Share the weather risk, the chance of meeting the date and the contingency plans
B) Confirm the date, since the investors need certainty to plan their own finances and loans
C) Avoid discussing the weather, since it is outside the project's control
D) Promise an earlier date to build in a buffer against bad weather
= A
? Expectations about uncertain dates are managed with transparent information about the risks, probabilities and plans.

@@ PE5 | Hybrid
Marketing has announced a launch date for a new product, but recipe trials on the new production line may need several iterations. What should the project manager do?
A) Agree early on the trial plan and a realistic launch window, with decision points
B) Ask the production team to finish all of the trials in a single iteration
C) Let marketing keep its date and hope that the first recipe trial on the new line is successful
D) Stop the trials and launch the product with the current recipe
= A
? Aligning expectations early on uncertain work, with clear decision points, prevents commitments that cannot be met.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
The event organiser expects the venue to be handed over two weeks before the tournament, but the contract says one week. What should the project manager do?
A) Clarify the contract and discuss an earlier handover as a possible change
B) Agree to the two weeks, since the organiser is an important client of the construction firm
C) Insist on one week and refuse to discuss the matter any further
D) Wait until handover and explain the contract terms at that point
= A
? Expectations are aligned against the contract. A different requirement can be considered through the change process.

@@ PE5 | Agile
Law firm partners expect a full feature list with fixed prices before any work starts, as in their usual engagements. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain the agile approach and offer a fixed budget and timebox with prioritised scope
B) Write a full feature list and fixed prices, since that is what the partners expect from projects
C) Refuse to start the project until the partners have accepted a fully agile approach without limits
D) Agree a fixed price for everything and hide the uncertainty in a large contingency
= A
? Expectations are aligned by explaining how agile delivery manages cost and scope, and by offering a structure, such as fixed budget and timebox, that gives the partners control.

@@ PE5 | Hybrid
The client believes that, because the team is agile, new requests will cost nothing extra. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain that change is welcome but replaces other work, or needs more funding
B) Agree that changes are free, since agile methods are designed to respond to change at no extra cost
C) Refuse all changes, since the scope was agreed when the agile project started
D) Accept the changes and hide the extra cost in the project's contingency reserve
= A
? Agile makes change easier, not free. Each change replaces other work within the same budget, or requires extra funding.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
The club expects premium seating, but the approved budget covers only standard seats. What should the project manager do?
A) Show the cost difference and let the club decide
B) Order premium seats and ask for the extra money once they have been installed
C) Install standard seats without telling the club about the difference
D) Reduce the number of seats so that premium ones fit within the budget
= A
? Expectation gaps between grade and budget are made visible, so that the decision-maker can choose with full information.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
Residents near the onshore substation complain about construction noise at night. What should the project manager do?
A) Investigate, reduce or reschedule noisy work, and keep residents informed
B) Continue the work as planned, since the construction permit allows work at night
C) Offer the residents free electricity once the wind farm starts to produce power
D) Ask the residents to send their complaints in writing to the local council
= A
? Complaints are addressed by investigating, taking reasonable action and keeping the people affected informed.

@@ PE6 | Agile
Lawyers complain that the tool changes every two weeks and that they cannot keep up. What should the product owner do?
A) Bundle user-facing changes into predictable releases with clear release notes
B) Stop releasing changes until the lawyers say that they are ready for the next release
C) Release changes every day instead, so that each one is smaller and easier to absorb
D) Ignore the complaints, since frequent releases are a key principle of agile delivery
= A
? Deployment frequency and user-facing release frequency can differ. Predictable releases with support help users keep up.

@@ PE6 | Hybrid
A major retailer is upset that a new product will reach its shelves a month late. What should the project manager do?
A) Inform the retailer early, explain why, and agree a revised plan such as a phased launch
B) Wait until the delay is certain before telling the retailer about the new delivery date
C) Blame the equipment supplier when explaining the delay to the retailer
D) Offer the retailer a large discount before understanding what it needs
= A
? Early, honest communication and joint replanning maintain trust with key customers.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
A sponsor complains that the redesigned stadium gives its logo less visibility than it was promised. What should the project manager do?
A) Check the commitments in the sponsorship agreement and work with the sponsor on a solution
B) Explain that the stadium's design is final and cannot be changed for any sponsor
C) Move the logo to the most visible place in the stadium without checking other agreements
D) Ask the club's legal department to handle the complaint without involving the project team at all
= A
? Stakeholder concerns are checked against agreed commitments, and solutions are developed together.

@@ PE6 | Agile
Stakeholders have stopped giving feedback, saying that nothing ever happens with it. What should the product owner do?
A) Show them what was done with their feedback, and why, at each review
B) Ask the stakeholders to submit their feedback through a formal form
C) Stop asking for feedback, since the stakeholders no longer want to give it
D) Promise to implement every piece of feedback in the next sprint
= A
? Closing the feedback loop, by showing what was done and explaining what was not, keeps stakeholders engaged.

@@ PE6 | Hybrid
After a new project manager takes over, the client's main contact seems less satisfied. What should the new project manager do?
A) Meet the client contact early to understand his expectations and build the relationship
B) Continue exactly as the previous project manager did, without discussing anything with the client
C) Ask the sponsor to explain to the client why the project manager changed
D) Wait for the client contact to raise any concerns at the next steering committee
= A
? A change of project manager disrupts relationships. Early, personal engagement rebuilds trust and clarifies expectations.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
A small hydraulic oil spill occurs from a vessel during installation. How should the project manage the regulator's expectations?
A) Report it as required, clean it up, investigate and share the corrective actions
B) Clean it up quietly, since the spill was small and caused no visible damage at sea
C) Report it only if the regulator asks about it during the next scheduled inspection
D) Ask the vessel owner to deal with the regulator, since the vessel caused the spill
= A
? Transparency and prompt action, as required by the permits, maintain the regulator's trust after an incident.

@@ PE7 | Predictive
The offshore installation runs in several campaigns over two years. How should lessons be used?
A) Capture them after each campaign and apply them to the next one
B) Capture them only at the end of the whole installation programme
C) Keep them in the memory of the experienced crew members
D) Use them only for the company's next wind farm project
= A
? Capturing lessons at natural break points and applying them immediately improves performance within the same project.

@@ PE7 | Agile
Legal rules built into the tool need regular updates as the law changes. How should this knowledge be managed?
A) Make named lawyers responsible for reviewing and updating the rules through a clear process
B) Let the developers update the rules whenever they read about a change in the law in the news
C) Update the rules once a year, during the annual review of the tool's performance
D) Leave the rules unchanged, since users will notice if anything becomes out of date
= A
? Codified knowledge needs clear owners and a maintenance process so that it stays accurate.

@@ PE7 | Hybrid
Which method best helps line operators learn and follow complex changeover procedures?
A) Short video work instructions available at the machine
B) A thick printed manual stored in the supervisor's office
C) A one-off presentation by the equipment vendor
D) An email with the procedures sent to each operator
= A
? Instructions that are visual, short and available at the point of use support both learning and consistent execution.

@@ PE7 | Agile
Developers lack legal domain knowledge, which slows down refinement. What should be done?
A) Have lawyers work closely with the team, for example in refinement and pairing sessions
B) Send the developers on a three-year law degree course before they continue the work
C) Ask the lawyers to write detailed specifications so that the developers need no knowledge
D) Let the developers learn the law on their own by reading the legislation
= A
? Close collaboration with domain experts transfers knowledge in context, where it is immediately useful.

@@ PE7 | Predictive
Which practice helps engineers gain a broad understanding of different parts of a project?
A) Job rotation
B) Resource levelling
C) Fast tracking
D) Gold plating
= A
? Job rotation moves people through different roles, which spreads knowledge and builds flexibility.

@@ PE7 | Hybrid | multi
Which two practices make lessons learned easier to find and use? (Choose two.)
A) Tagging lessons by topic and project type
B) Writing them as specific, actionable recommendations
C) Storing them in personal folders
D) Writing long narratives without conclusions
E) Recording them only verbally in meetings
= A, B
? Lessons are useful when they can be found and when they say clearly what to do differently.

@@ PE8 | Predictive | fill
How many communication channels are there in a project team of 25 people, including the project manager? (Answer with a number.)
= 300
? Channels = n(n − 1)/2 = 25 × 24 / 2 = 300.

@@ PE8 | Predictive
Offshore operations involve vessels, cranes and divers. How should daily communication be organised?
A) Through scheduled operational calls and agreed radio protocols
B) Through informal mobile phone calls between whoever needs to talk at the time
C) Through a weekly email that summarises the operations of the past week
D) Through the vessel captains, who decide what information to share with others
= A
? High-risk operations need structured, reliable communication with agreed protocols and schedules.

@@ PE8 | Agile
During a sprint review, a developer plans to show screenshots that contain real client names. What should happen?
A) Use anonymised data in the demo, as client confidentiality requires
B) Show the screenshots, since the review audience is internal staff
C) Show the screenshots, but ask the audience not to take notes
D) Cancel the demo, since no data can ever be shown at a review
= A
? Confidentiality obligations apply to all communications, including internal demos. Anonymised data protects clients while allowing a realistic demo.

@@ PE8 | Hybrid
The board asks for a one-page project update. How should it be structured?
A) The key message and decisions needed first, followed by brief supporting facts
B) A detailed chronology of everything that happened since the last update
C) The full risk register and issue log, so that the board has all of the information
D) Technical details first, so that the board understands the team's challenges
= A
? Executive communication leads with the main message and the decisions needed, supported by only the essential facts.

@@ PE8 | Predictive
On tournament days, hundreds of staff and volunteers must coordinate. What should the communications plan define?
A) Channels, call signs, command structure and escalation routes for event days
B) The colours of the staff and volunteer uniforms for each day of the tournament
C) The list of journalists who will attend the matches and the press events
D) The menu for the staff canteen during the days of the tournament
= A
? Event-day communication must be clear and robust, with defined channels, roles and escalation routes.

@@ PE8 | Agile
Many stakeholders cannot attend sprint reviews but want to know what is happening. What should the product owner do?
A) Send a short, regular product update with links to recorded demos
B) Invite them to the daily scrum, so that they hear progress every day
C) Send them the full sprint backlog and all of the team's task details
D) Wait until the release is complete and present everything at once
= A
? A short, regular update with optional recordings keeps a wider audience informed without overloading them.

@@ PE8 | Hybrid
Where should stakeholders' preferred communication methods and frequencies be documented?
A) In the communications management plan
B) In the work breakdown structure
C) In the risk register
D) In the procurement statement of work
= A
? The communications management plan records who needs what information, when, how and from whom.
